<https://martial.is/entity/01KZKS3XCJ5V6B3XBAYVC4NM4V>
        rdf:type                        martialis:Source;
        schema:archivedAt               <https://web.archive.org/web/20260414215347/https://fightingarts.com/okinawas-bushi-karate-gentlemen/>;
        schema:datePublished            "2024";
        schema:description              "Essay by Christopher Caile (published May 30, 2024) on the Okinawan concept of \"Bushi\" as distinct from the Japanese warrior code of Bushido. Caile consulted his sensei Katsuhiko Shinzato - professor of linguistics at Okinawa International University - who explained that \"Bushi\" uses the same kanji and pronunciation in Okinawa and Japan but different meanings: in Okinawa it was an honorific for respected karate masters rather than a warrior-class term. Footnote 3 records that Shinzato is the head of the Okinawan Karate-Do Shorin-Ryu Kishaba Juku and was a student of Tsunetaka Shimabukuro, Shoshin Nagamine, Seigi Nakamura, Chokei Kishaba and Chogi Kishaba (Yamani-Ryu Bojutsu). The article also touches on the lineage of Bushi Matsumura, Chotoku Kyan and others, and thanks Shinzato, Morio Higaonna and Walter Dailey for input."@en;
        schema:inLanguage               "en";
        schema:keywords                 "article";
        schema:keywords                 "matsubayashi-ryu";
        schema:keywords                 "shorin-ryu";
        schema:keywords                 "karate";
        schema:name                     "Okinawa's Bushi: Karate Gentlemen - Christopher Caile's Fighting Arts";
        schema:publisher                <https://martial.is/entity/01KZKS3XCJ5V6B3XBAYVC4NM4V/publisher>;
        schema:url                      <https://fightingarts.com/okinawas-bushi-karate-gentlemen/>;
