<https://martial.is/entity/01M07HCGJG0BSRWW7JTNKRH8Y5>
        rdf:type                       martialis:Person;
        schema:alternateName           "Phoebe Parry"@en;
        schema:alternateName           "Phoebe Hirano"@en;
        schema:alternateName           "Phoebe Laughton-Parry de Castro Henriques"@en;
        schema:alternateName           "Phoebe Laughton-Parry"@en;
        schema:birthDate               "1887-10-22";
        schema:birthPlace              <https://martial.is/entity/01M07HCGJG0BSRWW7JTNKRH8Y5/birthEvent/birthPlace>;
        schema:deathDate               "1937-10";
        schema:deathPlace              <https://martial.is/entity/01M07HCGJG0BSRWW7JTNKRH8Y5/deathEvent/deathPlace>;
        schema:description             "Welsh-born pioneer of women's jujutsu, among the first female instructors of the art in Britain. Born Phoebe Parry\nin Monmouthshire, she was adopted by the Roberts family, London manufacturing chemists, and changed her name. At\n16 she joined Uyenishi's Golden Square School of Jujutsu in 1903 and was promoted to instructor within a year of\ntraining. From December 1904 she performed alongside her teachers Yukio Tani and Sadakazu Uyenishi, including the Caxton Hall\ndemonstration with Emily Diana Watts (December 1904), the 1905 Aldershot army demonstrations, a June 1905 Japan Society\ngarden party at the Royal Botanic Gardens, and a November 1905 Marlborough Hall demonstration alongside Kanaya, Tani,\nMiyake, Huji and Eida. In 1906 she appeared in Sporting Life demonstrating alongside Miyake, Hirano, Yamashita and\nOmura at the headquarters of the Japanese School of Ju-Jitsu. Featured in Sandow's Magazine, Health and\nStrength, and The Lady's Realm, and shown as 'ladies instructor' in Tani and Miyake's The Game of Ju-Jitsu (1906)\nat the Japanese School of Ju-Jitsu, 305 Oxford Street. Married fellow instructor Juzo (Juso) Hirano (the Japanese\njujutsuka known here as Hirano Yuzo) in March 1907; the couple performed at the Palace Theatre, Manchester in 1908, where Phoebe was\nbilled as 'Miss Roberts, Lady Champion Ju-jitsu Wrestler of the World', before touring Spain with other instructors\nand teaching women's self-defence in Barcelona on 31 July 1908. Followed Hirano to his Barcelona Ju-jitsu Academy\n(1909) and then to Portugal; after Hirano drowned off Santa Cruz beach in 1915 she remarried, as Phoebe\nLaughton-Parry, Carlos de Castro Henriques in 1916. She died in Porto in October 1937.\n"@en;
        schema:keywords                "united-kingdom";
        schema:keywords                "portugal";
        schema:keywords                "women-jujutsu";
        schema:keywords                "jujutsu";
        schema:keywords                "pioneer";
        schema:name                    "Phoebe Roberts"@en;
