        schema:description              "Japanese Wikipedia article on Jikishin Kage-ryū kenjutsu (formally Kashima Shinden Jikishin Kage-ryū). Covers the disputed foundership (Matsumoto/Sugimoto Bizen-no-kami the traditional first head; the name dating from the seventh head Yamada Mitsutoki, 1683), the early adoption of shinai and bogu uchi-komi training, the widest spread of any school in late-Edo Japan, the branches (Chōnaga-ha, Fujikawa-ha, Danno-ha, Otani-ha, and the Satsuma transmission Shin'ei-ryū), the five transmitted kata, and the Meiji masters including Sakakibara Kenkichi (Otani-ha). The history section is citation-flagged."@en;
